WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
We'll start in Slack where the team is
discussing a customer's missing discount.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.220
I'll give CodeRabbit the
Jira report and repository,

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.500
then follow the evidence from
the ticket into the code.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.160
We'll agree on a focused plan and inspect

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.840
the resulting fix, tests and pull request.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.760
Finally, I'll ask CodeRabbit
to remember the business rule

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.080
and check whether it can recall
that decision in a new conversation.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.400
In Slack, I open the conversation
about a customer's missing discount.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.500
The order has reached the minimum
quantity but the promotion hasn't applied.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.900
I ask CodeRabbit to investigate linking
the Jira report and naming the repository.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.660
The ticket contains the reported problem,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.940
expected behavior and acceptance criteria.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.720
For this first request, I want an
explanation with links to the evidence.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.000
I'll review that before
asking for a code change.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.520
When the response arrives,
I open the thread and follow

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.720
its references to the
ticket and implementation.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.940
The ticket describes an unexpired promotion

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.860
with a minimum of 10
items and 10% discount.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.940
9 items should not
qualify, 10 and 11 should.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.540
CodeRabbit points to a
comparison that requires

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.560
the quantity to be
greater than the minimum.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.740
Greater than 10 excludes 10 itself
which explains the missing discount.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.940
I follow the code link
and check that condition.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.620
The response also identifies
a test for that exact

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.060
minimum that's marked
as an expected failure.

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.980
The test records the known problem.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.920
At this stage, CodeRabbit has
inspected the code and test.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.340
We haven't established a
passing result for a fix.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.020
The ticket and implementation now give
us a specific boundary to correct.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.580
Back in the thread, I ask for a plan.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.720
The minimum must be inclusive so an
order of exactly 10 items qualifies.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:50.340
I want a small change with tests
below, at and above the boundary.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.160
The existing expected failure test
should become a normal passing test.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.280
I also ask the plan to
preserve the discount

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.320
percentage and expiration behavior.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:04.560
At the exact expiration time, the
promotion should remain expired.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.280
I read the proposed approach and
check it against those requirements.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.820
The plan gives us a defined scope
and a way to judge the result.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.040
With that agreed, we are ready
to request implementation.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.340
I ask CodeRabbit to implement
the plan in a demo repository,

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.880
run the relevant checks and open
a pull request on a new branch.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.060
The response links to a dedicated
code channel for the implementation.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.840
I follow it to inspect the changed files

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.540
and validation, then open the pull request.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.160
The patch includes the inclusive comparison

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.480
and regression tests for the
quantity and expiration boundaries.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.680
In this recorded run, CodeRabbit reports

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.160
65 passed tests and 5
unchanged expected failures.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.340
GitHub's validation checks passed and
CodeRabbit approved the pull request.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:57.100
The delivery message links the result
back to Jira with the validation summary.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.340
The ticket and pull request remain open,

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:01.840
ready for the team to review
and decide what to accept.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.620
I return to Slack and ask
CodeRabbit to remember the promotion

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.860
rule and the regression cases
future changes should cover.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.260
The response identifies a
saved knowledge-base record

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:15.180
containing the inclusive
minimum, boundary tests

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.080
and the requirement to preserve
discount and expiration behavior.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:21.440
Its live remote check was
unavailable in this run,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.460
so I also ask recall in a new conversation.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.900
I ask which promotion rule
we agreed on and which tests

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.920
a future change should include
without repeating the answer.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:35.480
CodeRabbit returns the inclusive rule,

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:40.980
the 9, 10 and 11 item cases and
a reference to the saved record.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:44.900
We followed a customer report
from Slack into the evidence,

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.900
agreed on a fix and inspected its delivery.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:53.160
The team also brings that business
rule into its next conversation.
